    "body": "## AI Disclosure\r\nThis PR was almost entirely vibe-coded over the course of a few days.  I've put some effort into cleaning it up, but it's definitely not in a great shape at the moment.  I'm mostly opening up this (draft) PR to get the code out there, not as an intent to ship it!\r\n\r\n# Description\r\n\r\nThis PR cross-compiles the core Neovim engine to WebAssembly with Emscripten and runs it two ways: headless under Node.js, and in the browser behind a small demo page. Everything is additive and `EMSCRIPTEN`-guarded — the native build is untouched, and all native tests pass unchanged.\r\n\r\nTry it: download the `nvim-wasm-demo` artifact from the `wasm` workflow, unzip, `python3 -m http.server`, open the page, click the grid, type.\r\n\r\n## What's here (one commit per layer)\r\n\r\n1. **build(wasm): cross-compile the bundled deps with Emscripten** — libuv (patched to use its portable `poll(2)` backend), PUC Lua 5.1 (LuaJIT can't target wasm), lpeg, luv, tree-sitter + parsers, unibilium, utf8proc → `.deps-wasm/usr`, all `-fPIC` with wasm-native setjmp/longjmp.\r\n2. **wasm: cross-compile Neovim and run it headless under Node** — a normal two-stage cross build (the native `build/` supplies codegen via the existing `NLUA0_HOST_PRG` support). `node build-wasm/bin/nvim.js -- …` runs the real editor; the libuv event loop \"blocks\" by suspending via JSPI. MEMFS + NODEFS keeps fd 0/1 virtual so a postMessage channel can back them when the engine runs in a worker.\r\n3. **test: run the functional test suite against the wasm build** — the upstream functional suite is the port's test suite. A Node shim (`wasm/test/nvim`) is a drop-in `$NVIM_PRG`; `exec_lua` ships source instead of LuaJIT bytecode under `NVIM_TEST_WASM`; capability skips (`t.is_wasm()`) cover what the sandbox lacks (child processes, PTYs, sockets, threads, watchers). Native runs are unaffected — the spec changes are pure insertions.\r\n4. **wasm: tree-sitter** — bundled grammars statically linked and registered via `vim._ts_add_language_builtin`; third-party grammar `.wasm` side modules (`tree-sitter build --wasm`) dlopen at runtime via `-sMAIN_MODULE=2`, with an optional host-fetch hook so a browser page can `fetch()` parsers on demand.\r\n5. **wasm: package the runtime for the browser** — `file_packager` data packages in three variants (full/core/minimal) over one runtime-agnostic `nvim.wasm`, each gated on booting clean before it ships.\r\n6. **wasm: browser demo** — the engine in a Web Worker, driven by a pure-JS msgpack-RPC client and rendered by a `<pre>`-based DOM renderer (headless `Screen` model + `neovim-ui-pre.js`). postMessage transport ⇒ no COOP/COEP; deploys on any static host.\r\n7. **wasm: Node smoke tests** — a worker_thread engine host mirroring the browser worker, an e2e test that drives the real engine through the compiled browser library (attach, redraw decode, highlights, clipboard provider, teardown), and a tree-sitter test covering bundled + dynamic + host-fetched grammars.\r\n8. **ci(wasm)** — one workflow: build (with the boot gate), run the smoke tests + a functional-suite sample against the wasm engine, and upload the demo as a downloadable `nvim-wasm-demo.zip` artifact.\r\n\r\n## Capabilities / limitations\r\n\r\nWorks: the full editor — `vim.api`, Lua (PUC 5.1), the bundled runtime, `--embed` RPC, tree-sitter highlighting, real file editing under Node (NODEFS), MEMFS editing in the browser.\r\n\r\nNot available in the wasm sandbox (degrades exactly like any platform without the capability): child processes, sockets, fs-watching, threads.\r\n\r\n## Test results\r\n\r\n- Native functional suite: unchanged (the test-infra commit is inert without `NVIM_TEST_WASM`; verified against a baseline sweep in the same environment).\r\n- Wasm functional sweep (`wasm/test/triage.sh`, per-file): ~435/467 spec files green; the rest are capability skips' long tail tracked as known wasm-only issues (shada mpack decode in the harness, strptime semantics, async-parse timing, …).\r\n- `wasm/web` Node tests: all green. Demo verified interactively in Chrome.\r\n\r\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\r\n",
